All times in microseconds; mean ± sd of the per-run percentile over 3 runs. Top row of each table is publishing at 10 Hz, bottom 100 Hz.
**Caution: nng READ/QUERY latency is bimodal, so a mean ± sd over 3 runs describes neither mode** (an nng "mean" of 137 µs is one slow-mode run
averaged with fast ones; no run is near it). The per-run view comes first.

### READ p50 per run (reps 1 / 2 / 3)

![READ p50 per run](img/latency_runs.png)

{{table:read_runs}}

nng fast mode: p50 about 88-105 µs, about 9,000-10,000 READ/s (below zmq's 108-111 µs). nng slow mode: p50 about 145-162 µs, about 6,300-6,700 READ/s (about 45% above zmq).
Some runs are in between (e.g. 128 µs, 114 µs). In the committed data the slow mode appears only at 100 Hz (reps 1 and 3 at N = 10, 100, 800, rep 1 and partly rep 3 at N = 50), but a re-run
at 10 Hz also hit it, so the publish rate is not the cause. The cause is unknown (candidates: nng's aio/thread scheduling, Docker Desktop VM scheduling); not investigated.
Reps are the outer loop of the matrix, so a slow period of the host would hit the same rep for several cells, which fits the pattern but is not proven.

