Reading the tables:

- Medians sit at about 90-160 µs for both and barely move with N (except QUERY at 800 tags: about 140-190 µs) (READ looks up one tag; only QUERY grows, because its reply is 10 KB at 800 tags, 9 packets).
  With a bridge between client and provider, most of the 95 µs is the network stack, not the messaging library.
- In the fast mode nng's median is lower than zmq's; in the slow mode it is about 45% higher. Means over the 3 runs give nng/zmq READ p50 of 0.88-0.95 at 10 Hz and 1.03-1.25 at 100 Hz
  (QUERY 0.88-0.93 and 0.99-1.27), but that split follows which runs hit the slow mode, not the publish rate.
- The tail is worse on nng even in the fast mode: READ p99 at 10 Hz is 1.3-1.4x zmq's (214-226 µs vs 160-163 µs; 800 tags 217 vs 163 µs). At 100 Hz, 800 tags: 433 µs vs 279 µs (zmq's 800-tag p99 also rises with 100 Hz publishing).
- All maxima are ms-scale outliers (scheduler hiccups in a shared VM) and have large run-to-run spread; do not read anything into them.
- Write-to-visible is 10 ms for both (the engine tick). zmq's p95/p99 are a couple of ms higher than nng's here (about 12.3-13.7 vs 10.1-10.3 ms p95, at both publish rates, so nng is better here); the cause was not investigated.

## 2. Publishing

![publish delivery](img/publish_delivery.png)

![Publish cycles lost at 800 tags, ZeroMQ vs NNG](../img/charts/05a-nng-loss.svg)

*Charts: plotted numbers and the same charts with context in [results.md](../results.md).*

{{table:pub_delivery}}

- Both deliver 10.00 and 100.0 cycles per second at every size, with the same payload per cycle.
- **zmq (UDP RADIO) lost no cycles in any run.** On a bridge inside one VM that says nothing about a congested real network: UDP has no retransmit and no back-pressure.
- **nng (TCP PUB0) at 800 tags: 3.5-22% of cycles arrived incomplete in all 6 runs (12.7-22.0% at 10 Hz, 3.5-6.7% at 100 Hz); zmq at most one window-edge cycle per run.** TCP did not lose these; the publisher's own PUB0 send queue is the suspect (17 chunks per cycle vs a default queue of 16). At 10, 50 and 100 tags (1-3 chunks per cycle) nng delivered every cycle
  (the 0.1% cells are a single boundary cycle at a window edge).
- Receiving a whole cycle takes about 3x longer on nng at 100-800 tags and more at 50 (spread p50 at 800 tags: 396 µs zmq vs 1,247 µs nng): TCP segments and ACKs per message instead of back-to-back datagrams.

{{table:pub_timing}}

- Inter-arrival jitter is the provider's publish scheduler (the 5 ms sleep slices), about 2.8 ms sd at 10 Hz and 3.3 ms at 100 Hz, identical for both transports. Age above floor is also the same within noise: it is ±1-2 ms, so it cannot resolve sub-millisecond transport differences and says nothing about them.

## 3. Network traffic

![publish traffic](img/traffic_publish.png)

![Packets per publish cycle, ZeroMQ vs NNG](../img/charts/05c-nng-packets.svg)

*Charts: plotted numbers and the same charts with context in [results.md](../results.md).*

### Publish only (30 s window, no requests)

{{table:net_pub:nosd}}

- Bytes/cycle at 800 tags: 24.2 KB zmq vs 24.8 KB nng for the same ~21 KB of payload (zmq: 58 B of Ethernet, IP, UDP and RADIO-group headers per datagram; nng: headers plus TCP options plus the return ACKs).
- At 10 tags a whole cycle is one 284-byte frame on zmq; nng sends the data frame plus an ACK (366-410 B, 2 frames). That ratio shrinks as chunks fill the MTU.
- Packets per cycle equal chunks per cycle on zmq (1.00 at N=10 up to 17.3); on nng it is chunks + ACKs (25.5 at N=800: TCP delays ACKs, so about one ACK per two data segments).
- Exception: nng at 100 Hz, 10 tags shows 208 pkts/s because rep 2 of that cell had a burst of extra packets and 25% CPU (see runs.csv); the other two reps are at 200 pkts/s and 1.1-1.4% CPU. It is left in the means.
- Bytes on the wire, 800 tags: 242 KB/s zmq vs 248 KB/s nng at 10 Hz; 2.43 vs 2.50 MB/s at 100 Hz.

![request traffic](img/traffic_request.png)

### Request/reply, bytes and packets per request+reply pair (both directions, L2-L4 included)

{{table:net_req:nosd}}

- A READ costs 170-230 B and 2 packets on either (nng about 16 B more per request); a QUERY of 800 tags is a 10 KB reply in 9 packets on either.
  Request/reply wire cost is equal for practical purposes.

### Total bytes on the wire per second (request/reply + publish + other), by phase

{{table:net_total:nosd}}

- The request phases are closed loops (the client sends as fast as replies return), so these show what a saturating client costs: about 1.6-4.3 MB/s of READ requests, 2.3-67 MB/s of QUERY requests, versus 2.8 KB/s - 2.5 MB/s of publishing.
  A single field device polling at a sane rate is orders of magnitude below the loop numbers.

## 4. Provider CPU and memory

![cpu and rss](img/cpu_rss.png)

CPU is percent of one core, per phase (publish-only: engine plus publisher; the others add serving one closed-loop client).

{{table:cpu}}

{{table:rss}}

- Publish-only CPU is small on both and scales with N x rate; nng costs 15-60% more (800 tags at 100 Hz: 11.0% vs 17.3%), apart from the single outlier run above.
- Serving requests costs 28-32% (zmq) vs 48-58% (nng) of a core (the client is closed loop, so nng in its slow mode also completes fewer requests: see throughput).
- Memory is 4-6 MB on both and grows only with N.

## Conclusion

On this setup the two transports are close on speed and differ in behaviour. zmq has the steadier, safer profile today; nng has real advantages in
some cells but one defect that must be fixed first:

- Request latency: zmq is steady (READ p50 108-111 µs). nng is bimodal: lower median than zmq in its fast mode, about 45% higher in its slow mode, with a cause not yet known (ww-a8n note).
  nng's p99 is about 1.3-1.4x zmq's even at 10 Hz. zmq needs about 55-60% of nng's CPU to serve the same request stream.
- nng's write-to-visible p95/p99 are lower than zmq's (about 10.2 vs 12-14 ms); the cause was not investigated.
- Publish: nearly the same bytes on the wire (nng +2-10% at N >= 50), but nng sends 1.5-2x the packets (TCP ACKs) and takes about 3x longer to deliver a cycle's chunks.
- **nng dropped 3.5-22% of publish cycles at 800 tags**, zmq none. A client-side SUB0 buffer was ruled out in a separate test; the suspect is the provider's PUB0 send buffer.
  If NNG is to be used with models that large, the nng publisher needs a deeper send buffer (or pacing between chunks); that is a ferrofire change, bead ww-a8n, not measured here.

What the numbers do not say, and why the choice is not just about speed:

- **Delivery semantics differ.** nng PUB over TCP is connection-oriented: a slow or restarted subscriber reconnects, the stack retransmits lost segments, and bytes arrive in order, but a PUB0 socket still drops messages for a subscriber whose queue is full (as seen at 800 tags). zmq RADIO/DISH is UDP multicast: no
  delivery guarantee, no back-pressure, no ordering across datagrams, no per-subscriber state in the provider; it just works for one-to-many on a LAN and loses silently on a congested or lossy network. The zero loss here is a property of an idle in-VM bridge.
- **Interoperability.** Only the zmq transport talks to stock bennu field devices and bennu-probe; nng is for NNG clients only. That, not the 2x CPU, decides most real deployments.
- **Multicast vs unicast fan-out.** zmq's publish cost on the wire is independent of the number of subscribers (one multicast stream); nng PUB over TCP sends a copy per subscriber. This benchmark used a single subscriber, so it understates nng's cost with many.
- Single client, closed loop, one provider thread answering requests: these are latency and CPU-per-request numbers, not a multi-client capacity test.

## After the NNG fix (ww-a8n), historical (NNG dropped 2026-11-09)

*Everything above was measured on the code before the fix, and its raw data is unchanged in `raw/`. This section is {{after_runs}} new runs in
`docs/bench/results/raw-after-nng-fix/` (N = 100 as a control and 800; 10 and 100 Hz; 3 reps; both transports) on the fixed provider.*

The 800-tag loss was not network loss and TCP retransmission never applied: NNG's PUB0 is best-effort at the application layer and drops a message when
a subscriber pipe's send queue (`NNG_OPT_SENDBUF`, default 16) is full. ferrofire cut every publish cycle into ~1400-byte chunks (a UDP/MTU habit), so 800 tags
were 17 messages sent back to back against a queue of 16. The fix: on nng a cycle is **one message** (TCP segments it; no chunking), and the queue is
configurable (`nng_sendbuf`, default 128, 1..=8192; see the README). The zmq/UDP path is unchanged, so the zmq rows are a control: they should not move.
"messages/cycle" is what the subscriber received per cycle (zmq: datagrams of at most 1400 bytes; nng: before = chunks, after = 1). Loss is shown as mean (max over reps).

{{after_fix}}

Result: at 800 tags nng went from 5-22% loss to 0 in all 6 runs, with exactly 1 message per cycle, about 27% fewer packets per cycle (fewer TCP segments than 17 small sends) and about 2% fewer bytes. The zmq control did not move.
The one non-zero "after" cell is nng, 100 tags, 100 Hz, rep 3: 2,999 cycles received, all 2,999 complete, against 3,000 expected, i.e. a window-edge cycle (the same ±1 cycle resolution noted under Method), not a dropped message.

## Pull bridge prototype (ww-otj.11)

*Prototype, measured separately from everything above (new raw data in `docs/bench/results/raw-pull/`, 36 runs; nothing earlier was changed).*

Push sends every tag to every subscriber, and nng PUB0 over TCP has no multicast, so N RTUs cost N copies. The prototype tests the alternative
without touching bennu: the provider answers a batched `READ=tag1,tag2,...` with one `ACK=tag:val,...`, and a `ferrofire pull-bridge` process next to each
field device asks for only that device's tags every tick and republishes the reply locally in the normal update format (see the README, "Pull instead of push").

Setup: model with 800 tags; R RTUs, each needing K tags (`sim.t` plus K-1 others, disjoint slices), at 10 and 100 Hz, 3 reps of 30 s per cell, same Docker bridge
and monitor as above. **pull** = R real `pull-bridge` processes (zmq or nng REQ to the provider, republishing on loopback to a local subscriber that plays the
field device); the provider publishes once per 10 s to nobody, so its work is the batched READs alone. **push** (nng only) = R SUB0 subscribers, each
receiving all 800 tags; zmq push is one multicast stream whatever R is, so the single-subscriber zmq numbers from the main matrix apply. "Provider link" is every
frame (request, reply, publish, ARP; L2-L4 included) seen on the provider's interface. Staleness is `age above floor` over all RTUs, as defined in Method.

| mode | transport | RTUs x tags | Hz | reps | provider link B/s | provider CPU % | loss % mean (max) | staleness p50 / p99 / max ms |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| pull | nng | 8x100 | 10 | 3 | 356,336 | 5.8 | 0.000 (0.000) | 2.5 / 9.2 / 11.5 |
| pull | nng | 8x100 | 100 | 3 | 3,560,044 | 25.1 | 0.000 (0.000) | 5.8 / 10.1 / 12.5 |
| pull | nng | 8x50 | 10 | 3 | 188,991 | 5.3 | 0.000 (0.000) | 6.9 / 8.6 / 15.0 |
| pull | nng | 8x50 | 100 | 3 | 1,883,764 | 21.2 | 0.000 (0.000) | 4.7 / 9.0 / 15.4 |
| pull | zmq | 8x100 | 10 | 3 | 362,898 | 5.2 | 0.000 (0.000) | 4.5 / 6.9 / 13.0 |
| pull | zmq | 8x100 | 100 | 3 | 3,565,859 | 21.7 | 0.000 (0.000) | 4.9 / 6.5 / 9.4 |
| pull | zmq | 8x50 | 10 | 3 | 196,121 | 4.3 | 0.000 (0.000) | 1.7 / 5.9 / 8.9 |
| pull | zmq | 8x50 | 100 | 3 | 1,888,925 | 15.1 | 0.000 (0.000) | 2.9 / 9.8 / 13.5 |
| push | nng | 8x100 | 10 | 3 | 1,956,140 | 4.4 | 0.000 (0.000) | 5.3 / 12.8 / 13.4 |
| push | nng | 8x100 | 100 | 3 | 19,614,046 | 15.2 | 0.007 (0.011) | 5.3 / 13.2 / 14.9 |
| push | nng | 8x50 | 10 | 3 | 1,956,783 | 4.1 | 0.000 (0.000) | 5.4 / 12.9 / 13.3 |
| push | nng | 8x50 | 100 | 3 | 19,620,786 | 16.3 | 0.011 (0.011) | 4.9 / 12.5 / 16.6 |

Reference, from the earlier single-subscriber runs (800 tags; zmq from `raw/`, nng from `raw-after-nng-fix/`; nng multiplied by R=8, which the measured push rows above confirm):

| push reference | Hz | provider publish B/s | provider CPU % | loss % | staleness p50 / p99 ms |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| push zmq (multicast, any subscribers) | 10 | 242,295 | 3.8 | 0.000 | 9.5 / 14.5 |
| push zmq (multicast, any subscribers) | 100 | 2,429,497 | 11.0 | 0.000 | 6.5 / 14.4 |
| push nng (1 subscriber x8) | 10 | 1,954,283 | 3.9 | 0.000 | 7.6 / 15.0 |
| push nng (1 subscriber x8) | 100 | 19,624,282 | 8.7 | 0.000 | 7.2 / 14.8 |

What it shows:

- **Loss is zero by construction and in the data**: no pull cell lost a cycle (REQ/REP flow control; a failed request is retried once, then skipped). nng push at 100 Hz showed 0.007-0.011% (one window-edge cycle, as in the earlier section).
- **Wire**: against nng push to 8 RTUs, pull sends about 10x fewer bytes at K=50 (1.9 MB/s vs 19.6 MB/s at 100 Hz) and about 5.5x fewer at K=100. Against zmq multicast, which is one copy for any number of RTUs, pull is about 22% less at K=50 but 47% *more* at K=100 (3.6 MB/s vs 2.4 MB/s, where the 8 RTUs together ask for all 800 tags and each request also carries its tag names). Pull's cost grows with RTUs x scan rate; multicast push's does not.
- **Provider CPU is higher for pull, not lower**: 15-25% of a core at 100 Hz (8 REQ streams at 100 Hz = 800 requests/s) against 11% for zmq push and 15-16% for nng push to 8 subscribers; at 10 Hz 4-6% vs 4%. Each request is a full round trip on the provider's single REP thread (not profiled further; the per-tag linear lookup in `READ` is one suspect).
- **Staleness is unchanged within noise** (p50 2-7 ms, p99 6-10 ms for pull; 5 ms and 13 ms for nng push; both small against the 10 ms engine tick): the bridge pulls fresh values and republishes immediately, so pull adds no scan-rate delay on top; the value is as old as the last engine tick plus one round trip. The scan rate is what bounds freshness (a 10 Hz pull can show a value up to 100 ms old to the device if the device scans in between), which this ageing measure does not capture.
- Writes were not exercised here (the field device still WRITEs to the provider as before).
- Not measured in the bench container: a stock bennu field device in front of the bridge and writes (both done on the live range, see the next subsection); the bridge's republish format is also tested against the RADIO framing and the nng subscriber in `tests/pull.rs`.

Reproduce: `MODE=pull python3 bench/run.py; MODE=push TRANSPORTS=nng python3 bench/run.py; python3 bench/pull_report.py`.

### Live check on the waterway-arm64 range (ww-otj.10)

*Real VMs, not the bench container: provider VM `waterway-provider` and RTU VMs `rtu-1` (10 tags) / `rtu-2` (8 tags) of the `waterway-arm64` experiment (aarch64 Linux guests, stock `bennu-field-device`, 10 Hz publish). Scripts in `bench/live/`. One 30 s capture per cell, not repeated, so treat the numbers as single samples.*

> **Evidence status (2026-11-10):** re-measured with 3 reps per cell and committed captures (`docs/bench/results/live/`), see section G of [datagram-v1-bench.md](datagram-v1-bench.md); the pull rows reproduce within 3 %. The numbers below are the original single samples.

Setup: the stock provider (`bennu-simulink-provider` + `simulinksolver`) cannot answer a batched `READ`, so it was replaced by `ferrofire run waterway.yaml` on the same endpoints (`tcp://172.16.1.2:5555`; publish `udp://*;239.0.0.1:40000` for push, `udp://127.0.0.1:40000` (nobody listening, no traffic) for pull). On each RTU the daemon and watcher were stopped, `config.xml` copied with only `<input><endpoint>` changed to `udp://127.0.0.1:40000`, `ferrofire pull-bridge --tags-from <copy> --publish udp://127.0.0.1:40000` started, and the stock `bennu-field-device --file <copy>` started. Traffic is a `tcpdump` on the provider's and RTUs' mgmt NIC (172.16.1.0/16) for `udp 40000 or tcp 5555`, frame bytes.

What worked, exactly:

- **A plain loopback unicast endpoint `udp://127.0.0.1:40000` works with stock bennu.** Its DISH binds `127.0.0.1:40000` and joins the group; the bridge's RADIO group hash (`9ceaac3fa29ea7c`) is derived from the endpoint string, so the bridge and the device agree as long as `--publish` is the identical string. A loopback multicast group was not needed.
- **Modbus tracks the process behind the bridge.** Input registers 30000-30009 on rtu-1 (`10.1.1.101:502`), sampled every 2 s from a fresh provider start: `boat.location` rose 3375 -> 12582 and `lock.height` 327 -> 896 (the lock filling after the boat entered), `lowersensor`/`locksensor` active flags switched 65/0 -> 0/65 at the right moment, at both 100 ms and 1000 ms scan. Same shape as the push run (boat 6143 -> 14384, lock 327 -> 983 over the same kind of window).
- **Writes still reach the provider.** `FC6` write of 65535 to rtu-2 holding register 40001 (`uppervalve.open`): the provider (`bennu-probe read`) read 0 before, 1 at t+2 s, then 0 again from t+3 s because the RTU's own logic rewrote the output on its next cycle (that is the RTU logic, not the bridge).
- **Restore.** `bennu-watcherd restart` alone does not start the device; the originals were brought back with `bennu-field-deviced --c restart --env default --file /etc/sceptre/config.xml`, after which Modbus reads on both RTUs returned live values from the (ferrofire push) provider on the stock multicast endpoint. Stopping the experiment discards the rest.

| Provider-link traffic, 2 RTUs (30 s) | Provider NIC, all udp/40000 + tcp/5555 | Per RTU NIC | Provider CPU (core %, 30 s of `/proc` utime+stime) |
|---|---|---|---|
| push, stock `bennu-simulink-provider` (10 Hz multicast) | 3555 B/s (udp) + 28 B/s (tcp) | 3.7 KB/s (the same one stream) | 1.0% (provider + solver) |
| push, `ferrofire run` (10 Hz multicast) | 3660 B/s (udp) + 48 B/s (tcp) | 3.8 KB/s | 1.4% |
| pull, `--rate 100` ms | **11665 B/s** (tcp) | 5.3 KB/s (rtu-2), 6.0 KB/s (rtu-1) | 2.1% |
| pull, `--rate 1000` ms | **1206 B/s** (tcp) | 0.55 KB/s (rtu-2), 0.65 KB/s (rtu-1) | 0.6% |

Reading: on this range multicast is one 3.6 KB/s stream no matter how many RTUs listen, and pull is one request/reply stream per RTU, so with 2 RTUs a 10 Hz pull costs 3.3x the bytes of push (the replies are tiny, so TCP/ZMTP framing dominates: about 32 packets/s per RTU) while a 1 Hz pull costs 0.34x. The field device's `cycle-time` is 1000 ms, so a 1 s scan is enough for these RTUs; break-even against the multicast stream is about 5-6 RTUs at 1 Hz and under 1 at 10 Hz. Provider CPU is again slightly higher for pull. The pull win on the wire only appears where push has no multicast (nng/TCP) or the RTUs need a small slice of many tags (see the bench numbers above). Caveats: one run per cell, the ferrofire waterway model plays one transit then idles (stock provider behaviour at the start of the run was not compared), pull-bridge and field device CPU on the RTUs were not separated, and nng was not exercised live (zmq only).

## Caveats

- Docker bridge inside a VM on one host: not a real network. No loss, jitter, MTU or multicast-snooping effects; absolute latencies include the VM's network stack and virtualised scheduling. Relative differences are the useful part.
- Client is a Rust process in its own container; it shares the 18 cores with the provider and the monitor.
- The packet monitor runs during every measured phase; its CPU is in its own process, not in the provider's accounting, but it shares the VM's cores.
- Request-phase wire counts have about 1% boundary error (monitor samples every ~250 ms and phase edges are interpolated); the publish-only counts are exact to the packet at the 30 s scale.
- 3 repetitions: standard deviations are from n=3, so they are crude, and for nng latency they hide two modes (use the per-run table). Maxima are dominated by outliers.
- Age above floor is relative, not an absolute one-way latency (the provider sends no timestamps and sleeps in 5 ms slices).
- The sections above measured the provider before the ww-a8n fix; the nng loss and the REP CPU difference are properties of that code, not necessarily of NNG itself. The "After the NNG fix" section re-measures N = 100 and 800 on the fixed code.
- The first full pass counted multicast frames twice (found by comparing packets per cycle with chunks per cycle); the zmq runs were repeated with the fix, and the nng runs from before the fix are kept (TCP is not affected).
  Two earlier smoke runs were discarded.

## Reproduce

```
make bench          # ~1.2 h, docker only; raw results land in docs/bench/results/raw/
make bench-report   # regenerates CSVs, charts and this file from the raw results
```

Files: `docs/bench/results/runs.csv` (one row per run), `docs/bench/results/aggregate.csv` (mean/sd/min/max per cell and metric), `docs/bench/results/raw/` (per run: client JSON, monitor CSV, provider log), `docs/bench/img/`.
